<% content_for :sidebar do %>
  <div class="sidebar-stack">
    <%= link_to "Back Home", "/achievements/", class: "sidebar-button" %>
  </div>
<% end %>

<section class="chain-index help-page">
  <header class="chain-index__header">
    <p class="eyebrow">Help</p>
    <h1>About Achievement Chains</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="chain-card">
    <h2>What is this?</h2>
    <p>
      Achievement Chains is a small project for turning Steam achievements into routes you can follow. Instead of staring at one huge achievement list, you can build or follow a chain: a path of milestones, notes, and goals for a game.
    </p>

    <h2>Why chains?</h2>
    <p>
      Some games have hundreds of achievements, and it is not always obvious what you should do first, what depends on what, or how to reach a final goal without getting lost. Chains make that journey more visual and a little more intentional.
    </p>

    <h2>Can I make my own?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes. You can make chains for yourself, share them with others, and track progress through the achievements in that chain.
    </p>

    <h2>What else is planned?</h2>
    <p>
      I would like to add community goals, saved or followed chains, favorites, comments, feeds, and better social features around achievement hunting.
    </p>

    <h2>Is this open source?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes. The source code is available at <a href="https://github.com/jane-natucci/achievements" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">jane-natucci/achievements</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Who made this?</h2>
    <p>
      I'm Jane, someone who likes Steam achievements and wanted them to feel more like meaningful journeys. For questions and suggestions you can find me <a href="https://github.com/jane-natucci" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
